course: biology
lesson: 76
title: The end of the dinosaurs, and birds as living dinosaurs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: About 66 million years ago a rock about 10 kilometres wide, taller than Mount Everest, slammed into the sea near Mexico; dust and gas blocked the sunlight, the world went cold and dark, and about three quarters of all kinds of living things died out.
explain: But the dinosaurs did not all vanish: one branch of small feathered meat-eaters survived, and their great-great-grandchildren scratch for worms in the farmyard today; birds are living dinosaurs.
explain: Pull the wishbone after a Sunday roast and you hold a bone T. rex had too; look at a pigeon's scaly feet and sharp claws and you are looking at a dinosaur's foot.
explain: In cartoons the dinosaurs all die at once in one fiery bang and small furry mammals were never there; in reality the dark years did the killing, volcanoes in India were erupting too, and mammals waited in their burrows for their turn.
fact: About 66 million years ago an asteroid roughly 10 kilometres wide hit what is now the Yucatan coast of Mexico, making the Chicxulub crater about 180 kilometres across.
fact: Dust and gas from the impact blocked sunlight, plants stopped growing, the plant-eaters starved and then the meat-eaters that ate them, and about three quarters of all kinds of animals and plants died out, including all dinosaurs except birds.
fact: In 1980 Luis and Walter Alvarez found a clay layer all over the world rich in iridium, a metal rare on Earth but common in asteroids; below it there are dinosaur fossils, and above it none, apart from birds.
fact: At the same time the Deccan Traps volcanoes poured lava across India for hundreds of thousands of years, and many scientists think their gases made things harder before and after the asteroid.
fact: Survivors included many small mammals, birds, crocodiles, turtles, frogs, snakes and lizards, which needed less food, could hide, and ate seeds, insects or rotting plants.
fact: Birds evolved from small feathered meat-eating dinosaurs called theropods, the group of T. rex and Velociraptor, and there are about 11,000 kinds of bird today.
fact: Bird legs and feet are covered in scales, like their dinosaur ancestors; owls and grouse also have feathers on their feet for warmth.
fact: The wishbone is two collarbones joined together; T. rex and Velociraptor had wishbones too, long before birds flew.
fact: Yutyrannus, a relative of T. rex from China about 125 million years old and about 9 metres long, had long fluffy feathers; feathers first evolved for warmth and display, and only later for flying.
fact: The ostrich is the biggest living bird, up to about 2.7 metres tall, and runs at around 70 kilometres per hour; it does not bury its head in sand; the myth may come from ostriches lowering their heads to turn their eggs in the nest hole.
fact: Many bird bones are hollow with thin struts and connect to air sacs for breathing, which makes them light but still strong for flying; giant sauropod dinosaurs had air sacs in their bones too.
fact: After the dinosaurs died out, mammals spread into many new kinds, and within about 10 million years there were early bats, early whales and dog-sized early horses.
fact: Crocodiles survived the asteroid too, and they are closer relatives of birds than of lizards.
exercise: Where did the asteroid hit 66 million years ago? | Near the Yucatan coast of Mexico | In the middle of the Sahara Desert | On the North Pole ice | In the centre of India
exercise: How wide was the asteroid, roughly? | About 10 kilometres | About 10 metres across | About 1,000 kilometres | About 100 metres across
exercise: How did the asteroid kill so much life far from the crater? | Dust and gas blocked the sunlight, so it got cold and plants died | The shock wave raced round the world and knocked over every animal on Earth | It set fire to every ocean | It made the Earth spin backwards
exercise: About how much of all kinds of animals and plants died out? | About three quarters | About one in a hundred | About half of one percent | Every single kind of animal
exercise: What clue did Luis and Walter Alvarez find in 1980? | A worldwide clay layer rich in iridium, a metal common in asteroids | A piece of the asteroid stuck inside a T. rex skull dug up in Mexico | Burnt footprints leading away from Mexico | Cave paintings of a falling star
exercise: Which other disaster was happening at the same time in India? | Huge volcanic eruptions called the Deccan Traps | A giant tsunami covering the whole country from coast to coast | An ice age that froze the jungles | A swarm of locusts eating all plants
exercise: Which of these animals survived the asteroid? | Crocodiles | Triceratops | The pterosaurs | The ammonites
exercise: Why might being small have helped animals survive? | Small animals needed less food and could hide in burrows | Small animals were too fast for the dust and simply outran the cold | Small animals could fly above the clouds | The asteroid missed small animals on purpose
exercise: From which group of dinosaurs did birds evolve? | Small feathered meat-eaters called theropods | Long-necked plant-eaters called sauropods | Armoured plant-eaters with clubs like Ankylosaurus | Flying reptiles called pterosaurs
exercise: What covers a pigeon's feet? | Scales, like its dinosaur ancestors | Smooth wet skin, like a frog's | Soft fur, like a mouse | Feathers, as on every bird
exercise: What is the wishbone? | Two collarbones joined together | An extra rib that birds have for luck | A bone from the end of the bird's tail | A bone found only in farm chickens
exercise: Which dinosaur also had a wishbone? | T. rex | Triceratops | Stegosaurus | Diplodocus
exercise: What was Yutyrannus? | A 9-metre relative of T. rex with long fluffy feathers | A tiny flying reptile with leathery wings and a long beak | A long-necked plant-eater from Africa | A sea reptile with flippers and a long neck like a snake's
exercise: Why did feathers first evolve? | For warmth and display, before flying | Only for flying, right from the very start | For swimming under water | To catch insects like a net
exercise: How fast can an ostrich run? | Around 70 kilometres per hour | Around 7 kilometres per hour, a slow walk | Around 200 kilometres per hour | It cannot run at all, only hop
exercise: Why are many bird bones hollow with struts inside? | To be light but still strong for flying | Because birds have no bone marrow at all inside | To store water for long flights | To make whistling sounds
exercise: What happened to mammals after the dinosaurs died out? | They spread and grew into many new kinds | They all died out soon after the dinosaurs did | They stayed exactly the same size | They turned into dinosaurs
test: «dinos are extinct, so birds can't be dinosaurs, duh» What is right? | Birds evolved from feathered theropods, so they are surviving dinosaurs | Birds came from pterosaurs, not from dinosaurs, so they are flying reptiles | Birds came from bats after the dinosaurs | Birds have always existed and never changed
test: In a cartoon, the asteroid lands and every dinosaur on Earth burns in one second. What is closer to the truth? | Most died in the cold, dark years after, when plants stopped growing | Every single dinosaur on the planet really did die within one second | Only the dinosaurs in Mexico died | The dinosaurs all drowned in the flood it made
test: «why'd the T. rex die if it ate meat, not plants?» What is the best answer? | Plants died without sunlight, then plant-eaters, then meat-eaters | T. rex secretly ate plants too, so it starved when the plants were gone | The asteroid hit every T. rex directly | Meat-eaters were allergic to dust
test: What would the world probably be like if the asteroid had missed? | Big dinosaurs might still rule and mammals might have stayed small | Exactly the same as today | There would be no birds or any reptiles | Humans would have appeared 60 million years earlier and tamed the dinosaurs
test: «ostriches hide their heads in the sand when scared» What is right? | That's a myth; they lower their heads to turn eggs in the nest hole | Yes, and they can breathe through the sand for hours | Yes, so the lions cannot see them | No, they hide their heads under their wings until the danger has gone away
test: «my mate says crocs are just big lizards» What is right? | Crocodiles are closer relatives of birds than of lizards | Yes, crocodiles are just giant lizards that live in water | No, crocodiles are a kind of fish | No, crocodiles are mammals
test: Why is a thin layer found all over the world such good evidence? | It shows one event touched the whole planet at the same time | It proves the asteroid was made of clay | It shows each country was hit by its own small asteroid at a different time | It shows the layer was painted on later
test: Which animal is the closest living relative of T. rex in this list? | A chicken | A lizard | A shark | A rhino
test: «why would a dino that can't fly have feathers, that's dumb» What is right? | Feathers first helped with warmth and display, and later with flying | Feathers are useless without flying, so they must all have flown | Only flying dinosaurs had feathers | Feathers grew only on dinosaur babies
test: «bloody huge bird, an ostrich, is that even a dinosaur» What is right? | Yes, like all birds it is a living dinosaur | No, only the small birds are dinosaurs | No, ostriches came from camels in the desert | Yes, but only the eggs are dinosaurs
test: Why could two disasters together, an asteroid and the volcanoes, be worse than one? | Life already weakened by one had less chance to survive the other | Two disasters cancel each other out | Volcanoes cooled down the asteroid crater | The ash from the volcanoes blocked the asteroid, but then made the whole world freeze
exam: Identify the evidence adduced by Luis and Walter Alvarez in the year 1980 in support of an asteroid impact. | A worldwide stratum of clay enriched in iridium, an element abundant in asteroids | A fragment of the asteroid embedded within a dinosaur skull | Trackways of dinosaurs leading away from Mexico | A layer of ice of worldwide extent containing great quantities of meteoric dust
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the evolutionary origin of birds? | Birds are descended from small feathered carnivorous dinosaurs | Birds are descended from the pterosaurs, the flying reptiles of the Mesozoic | Birds are descended from the long-necked sauropods | Birds arose independently of all reptiles
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the principal cause of death following the impact. | The obscuring of sunlight by dust, which cooled the planet and halted plant growth | The direct impact of the asteroid upon every living creature | A worldwide flood which drowned all land animals | A reversal of the rotation of the Earth
exam: State the anatomical composition of the wishbone, or furcula. | Two collarbones fused together | A single additional rib | A bone of the tail | A portion of the skull
